{"QuestionText": "<strong><font size=\"3\">Welcome to the&nbsp;practice module for the Neuroanatomy Lab Practicum!</font></strong><br><font size=\"3\">\nFor each question, I have indicated whether the brain area/feature is gray matter, white matter, a cavity, or a groove. I've provided below a more detailed explanation of these designations.</font>\n<div>\n<ul>\n\t<li><span style=\"font-size:16px;\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri, sans-serif\"><span style=\"caret-color:#000000\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-style:normal\"><span style=\"font-variant-caps:normal\"><span style=\"font-weight:normal\"><span style=\"letter-spacing:normal\"><span style=\"text-transform:none\"><span style=\"white-space:normal\"><span style=\"word-spacing:0px\"><span style=\"text-decoration:none\"><b><i>Gray matter</i></b><i>:&nbsp;</i>A group of cell bodies in the brain. May include one of the following terms: nucleus, body, gyrus.&nbsp;&nbsp;</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t<li><span style=\"font-size:16px;\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri, sans-serif\"><span style=\"caret-color:#000000\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-style:normal\"><span style=\"font-variant-caps:normal\"><span style=\"font-weight:normal\"><span style=\"letter-spacing:normal\"><span style=\"text-transform:none\"><span style=\"white-space:normal\"><span style=\"word-spacing:0px\"><span style=\"text-decoration:none\"><b><i>White matter</i></b><i>:&nbsp;</i>A fiber tract (group of axons).&nbsp;May include one of the following terms:&nbsp;&nbsp;tract, nerve, fasciculus (\u201clittle bundle\u201d), peduncle, capsule, or commissure.&nbsp;&nbsp;</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t<li><span style=\"font-size:16px;\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri, sans-serif\"><span style=\"caret-color:#000000\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-style:normal\"><span style=\"font-variant-caps:normal\"><span style=\"font-weight:normal\"><span style=\"letter-spacing:normal\"><span style=\"text-transform:none\"><span style=\"white-space:normal\"><span style=\"word-spacing:0px\"><span style=\"text-decoration:none\"><b><i>Cavities</i></b><i>:&nbsp;</i>Cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)-filled chambers in the brain may be called ventricles, aqueducts, or cisterns.</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t<li><span style=\"font-size:16px;\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri, sans-serif\"><span style=\"caret-color:#000000\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-style:normal\"><span style=\"font-variant-caps:normal\"><span style=\"font-weight:normal\"><span style=\"letter-spacing:normal\"><span style=\"text-transform:none\"><span style=\"white-space:normal\"><span style=\"word-spacing:0px\"><span style=\"text-decoration:none\"><b><i>Grooves</i></b><i>:</i>&nbsp;Grooves may be called sulci (singular: sulcus) or fissures.&nbsp;</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n</ul>\n<span style=\"font-size:16px;\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri, sans-serif\"><span style=\"caret-color:#000000\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-style:normal\"><span style=\"font-variant-caps:normal\"><span style=\"font-weight:normal\"><span style=\"letter-spacing:normal\"><span style=\"text-transform:none\"><span style=\"white-space:normal\"><span style=\"word-spacing:0px\"><span style=\"text-decoration:none\">You will have <u>1 minute</u> to answer each labeling question. After typing your answer, be sure to click on the right-facing arrow on the bottom right of the page to submit your answer and to see whether or not you were correct.</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><br></span></div>", "DefaultChoices": false, "QuestionType": "DB", "Selector": "TB", "Configuration": {"QuestionDescriptionOption": "UseText"}, "QuestionDescription": "Welcome to the\u00a0practice module for the Neuroanatomy Lab Practicum!For each question, I have indic...", "ChoiceOrder": [], "Validation": {"Settings": {"Type": "None"}}, "GradingData": [], "Language": [], "NextChoiceId": 4, "NextAnswerId": 1, "DataVisibility": {"Private": false, "Hidden": false}, "QuestionText_Unsafe": "<strong><font size=\"3\">Welcome to the&nbsp;practice module for the Neuroanatomy Lab Practicum!</font></strong><br><font size=\"3\">\nFor each question, I have indicated whether the brain area/feature is gray matter, white matter, a cavity, or a groove. I've provided below a more detailed explanation of these designations.</font>\n<div>\n<ul>\n\t<li><span style=\"font-size:16px;\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri, sans-serif\"><span style=\"caret-color:#000000\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-style:normal\"><span style=\"font-variant-caps:normal\"><span style=\"font-weight:normal\"><span style=\"letter-spacing:normal\"><span style=\"text-transform:none\"><span style=\"white-space:normal\"><span style=\"word-spacing:0px\"><span style=\"text-decoration:none\"><b><i>Gray matter</i></b><i>:&nbsp;</i>A group of cell bodies in the brain. May include one of the following terms: nucleus, body, gyrus.&nbsp;&nbsp;</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t<li><span style=\"font-size:16px;\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri, sans-serif\"><span style=\"caret-color:#000000\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-style:normal\"><span style=\"font-variant-caps:normal\"><span style=\"font-weight:normal\"><span style=\"letter-spacing:normal\"><span style=\"text-transform:none\"><span style=\"white-space:normal\"><span style=\"word-spacing:0px\"><span style=\"text-decoration:none\"><b><i>White matter</i></b><i>:&nbsp;</i>A fiber tract (group of axons).&nbsp;May include one of the following terms:&nbsp;&nbsp;tract, nerve, fasciculus (\u201clittle bundle\u201d), peduncle, capsule, or commissure.&nbsp;&nbsp;</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t<li><span style=\"font-size:16px;\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri, sans-serif\"><span style=\"caret-color:#000000\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-style:normal\"><span style=\"font-variant-caps:normal\"><span style=\"font-weight:normal\"><span style=\"letter-spacing:normal\"><span style=\"text-transform:none\"><span style=\"white-space:normal\"><span style=\"word-spacing:0px\"><span style=\"text-decoration:none\"><b><i>Cavities</i></b><i>:&nbsp;</i>Cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)-filled chambers in the brain may be called ventricles, aqueducts, or cisterns.</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t<li><span style=\"font-size:16px;\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri, sans-serif\"><span style=\"caret-color:#000000\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-style:normal\"><span style=\"font-variant-caps:normal\"><span style=\"font-weight:normal\"><span style=\"letter-spacing:normal\"><span style=\"text-transform:none\"><span style=\"white-space:normal\"><span style=\"word-spacing:0px\"><span style=\"text-decoration:none\"><b><i>Grooves</i></b><i>:</i>&nbsp;Grooves may be called sulci (singular: sulcus) or fissures.&nbsp;</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n</ul>\n<span style=\"font-size:16px;\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri, sans-serif\"><span style=\"caret-color:#000000\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-style:normal\"><span style=\"font-variant-caps:normal\"><span style=\"font-weight:normal\"><span style=\"letter-spacing:normal\"><span style=\"text-transform:none\"><span style=\"white-space:normal\"><span style=\"word-spacing:0px\"><span style=\"text-decoration:none\">You will have <u>1 minute</u> to answer each labeling question. After typing your answer, be sure to click on the right-facing arrow on the bottom right of the page to submit your answer and to see whether or not you were correct.</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><br></span></div>", "DataExportTag": "Q376", "QuestionID": "QID376"}}, {"SurveyID": "SV_e5qxdjKxDQL1V8q", "Element": "SQ", "PrimaryAttribute": "QID377", "SecondaryAttribute": "Choose which \"view\" you'd like to study. ", "TertiaryAttribute": null, "Payload": {"QuestionText": "Choose which \"view\" you'd like to study.&nbsp;", "DefaultChoices": false, "QuestionType": "MC", "Selector": "SAVR", "SubSelector": "TX", "Configuration": {"QuestionDescriptionOption": "UseText", "TextPosition": "inline"}, "QuestionDescription": "Choose which \"view\" you'd like to study. ", "Choices": {"1": {"Display": "Gross Neuroanatomy"}, "4": {"Display": "Midsagittal Neuroanatomy"}, "5": {"Display": "Coronal Neuroanatomy"}}, "ChoiceOrder": ["1", "4", "5"], "Validation": {"Settings": {"ForceResponse": "OFF", "Type": "None"}}, "GradingData": [], "Language": [], "NextChoiceId": 7, "NextAnswerId": 4, "DataVisibility": {"Private": false, "Hidden": false}, "QuestionText_Unsafe": "Choose which \"view\" you'd like to study.&nbsp;", "DataExportTag": "Q377", "QuestionID": "QID377", "QuestionJS": "Qualtrics.SurveyEngine.addOnload(function()\n\n{\n\n   jQuery(this.getQuestionTextContainer ()).css(\"padding-top\",\"0px\");\n\n\n\n   jQuery(this.getQuestionTextContainer ()).css(\"padding-bottom\",\"0px\");\n\n});"}}, {"SurveyID": "SV_e5qxdjKxDQL1V8q", "Element": "SQ", "PrimaryAttribute": "QID378", "SecondaryAttribute": "Welcome to the Sheep Brain Study Module!", "TertiaryAttribute": null, "Payload": {"QuestionText": "<h2><strong>Welcome to the Sheep Brain Study Module!</strong></h2>\n\n<script>Qualtrics.SurveyEngine.addOnReady(function(){\n\njQuery(\"#Footer\").hide(); \n\n});</script>", "DefaultChoices": false, "DataExportTag": "Q296", "QuestionID": "QID378", "QuestionType": "DB", "Selector": "TB", "Configuration